  "summary": "Fossil sunlight in resin — the gem the whole ancient world traded north for.",
  "description": "Baltic amber — tree resin fossilized over 40 million years, carrying insects, feathers and light — made the southeastern Baltic a magnet culture for ten millennia. Neolithic workshops on the Curonian Lagoon drilled beads for European trade; the Roman Empire's Amber Road linked it to Aquileia; and Teutonic, Hanseatic and Russian orders fought over rights to the sea's \"gold\". Amber became the material of local identity: smelted into prayer beads, Art Nouveau brooches, Soviet-era jewelry and healers' talismans, its glow is still used by Latvian and Lithuanian designers as the color of home itself.",
  "culturalContext": "Amber made the Baltic a magnet culture for ten millennia — Roman Amber Road, Teutonic monopolies, Hanseatic trade — and remains Latvia's and Lithuania's \"gold of the sea\": national gift economy, Art Nouveau heritage, healer's talisman and designer material, with Palanga's museum as its temple.",
